// Store-store forwarding may not cross an acquire, and RC11 does not
// forward (#207, #210).
//
// RC11 forbids r0 = 1 with x ending at 2: x := 2 is po-before the release
// store to flag, which synchronises with the acquire load, which is po-before
// the second x := 1. So x := 2 happens-before that store, coherence orders it
// first, and the last store to x is x := 1.
//
// sMRD's redundant-store elimination forwarded the first x := 1 into the
// second and dropped the second, across the acquire. MoRDor used to check
// RC11 against that execution too, where nothing orders x := 2 before a later
// store of thread 0, and RC11 admitted the outcome. sMRD forbade it only
// because the remapped ppo turned the acquire's pair (r0, second x := 1) into
// the backward pair (r0, first x := 1), which orders x := 2 before the first
// store.
//
// RC11 is asked about the program's own events only (#207). sMRD no longer
// forwards across an acquire (#210): dropping the second store hoists it above
// the acquire, which a compiler may not do. sMRD forbids the outcome with no
// backward ppo pair.
x := 0; flag := 0;
{
  x.store(1, rlx);
  r0 := flag.load(acquire);
  x.store(1, rlx)
} ||| {
  x.store(2, rlx);
  flag.store(1, release)
}
%%
forbid (r0 = 1 && @x = 2) [RC11, sMRD]
